---
import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro';
import { repo, services } from '../data';

const toc = [
  { id: 'data', label: 'Data about you' },
  { id: 'sign-in', label: 'Your sign-in' },
  { id: 'services', label: 'Services' },
  { id: 'search-history', label: 'Search history' },
  { id: 'notifications', label: 'Notifications' },
  { id: 'clipboard', label: 'Clipboard' },
  { id: 'website', label: 'This website' },
];
---

<Doc
  title="Privacy · Velvet"
  description="What data Velvet keeps on your Mac, which services it connects to, and what each service gets."
  label="Privacy"
  heading="What Velvet keeps and what it sends"
  lede="Velvet has no analytics, no telemetry and no crash reports."
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="data">Data about you</h2>
  <p>
    Velvet does not collect data about you. The maintainer of Velvet gets no data from the app. Velvet is open source.
    Thus, you can read the <a href={repo}>code</a> that does each function on this page.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sign-in">Your sign-in</h2>
  <p>
    You sign in to your Google account in a window that Velvet opens. Velvet then keeps your session cookies in a file
    in this folder:
  </p>
  <pre><code>~/Library/Application Support/com.subhash.Velvet</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li>The file is not encrypted.</li>
    <li>The permissions of the file let only your macOS user account read it.</li>
    <li>Other apps that run as your user can read the file.</li>
    <li>Backups of your Mac include the file.</li>
    <li>Velvet sends the cookies only to YouTube and Google.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>To remove the cookies, sign out in <strong>Settings › Account</strong>.</p>
  <p>
    Velvet does not use the macOS keychain for the cookies. Velvet has a self-signed signature, and with this type of
    signature macOS asks for your login password after each update.
  </p>
  <p>You can use Velvet without an account.</p>

  <h2 id="services">Services</h2>
  <p>Velvet connects only to these services:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Service</th><th>Data that Velvet sends</th><th>Why Velvet connects</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        services.map((s) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{s.name}</td>
            <td>{s.sends}.</td>
            <td>{s.why}.</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>You can disable each service in the settings, except YouTube, Google and GitHub.</p>
  <p>
    The DeArrow thumbnail service can see which videos you look at. To stop this, set <strong
      >Settings › DeArrow › Thumbnails</strong
    > to <strong>Original</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="search-history">Search history</h2>
  <p>
    When you are signed in, Velvet reads your search history from the Google My Activity page. This history comes from
    all your devices. Velvet only reads it.
  </p>
  <p>
    This function is on by default. You can turn it off in the welcome or in <strong>Settings › General</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="notifications">Notifications</h2>
  <p>
    Velvet can show a macOS notification for a new upload. To find new uploads, Velvet reads the notifications of your
    account. YouTube then marks them as seen on all your devices, as when you open the bell on youtube.com.
  </p>

  <h2 id="clipboard">Clipboard</h2>
  <p>
    Velvet can open a YouTube link that you copied. Velvet reads the clipboard only when it becomes the active app, and
    only when the clipboard contains a web address.
  </p>
  <p>
    On macOS 15.4 or later, macOS can tell you each time that Velvet reads the clipboard. To stop this function, go to
    <strong>Settings › General</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="website">This website</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>This website has no analytics and sets no cookies.</li>
    <li>The fonts and the scripts come from this website. Your browser gets nothing from other servers.</li>
    <li>
      Cloudflare is the host of this website. Cloudflare receives each request, with your IP address and the address of
      the page, as each web host does.
    </li>
    <li>The download buttons go to GitHub.</li>
    <li>
      A <a href="/open#website">link through this website</a> contains the ID of a video. The page sends this ID only to
      Velvet on your Mac.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Doc>
